course: dictionary
lesson: 14019
title: Magnified: bigger, louder, or more serious
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Magnified» is the past form of the verb magnify, so it describes something that has been made bigger, louder, or more serious than before. Look at the words around it to decide which meaning fits. Ask yourself: what was made bigger, or stronger, or more important?
explain: «The scientist looked at the cell through a microscope, and the cell was magnified 100 times.» A microscope makes tiny things look bigger, and «100 times» tells you how much bigger. So magnified means made to look bigger.
explain: «The news report magnified a small mistake until it sounded like a disaster.» Here nothing got physically bigger. The mistake was made to seem more serious than it really was. Magnified can mean exaggerated, so check the clues before choosing a meaning.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. If you swap in «made to seem more serious» and the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning. In church, «My soul magnifies the Lord» means praises the Lord.
fact: Magnify means to make something look bigger, often with a lens, microscope or magnifying glass.
fact: «Magnified» can also mean made louder or stronger, as in «The speaker's voice was magnified by the microphone».
fact: «Magnified» can mean exaggerated, made to seem more important than it really is.
fact: In church, to magnify means to praise, so «magnify the Lord» means praise God.
fact: Scientists often say «magnified × 10», which means ten times bigger than the real size.
exercise: «The scientist looked at the cell through a microscope, and the cell was magnified 100 times.» What does magnified mean here? | *Made to look bigger, so it seems larger | Made louder | Made to look sad | Made to look smaller
exercise: Dad says, «The news report magnified the problem, making a small mistake sound like a disaster.» What does magnified mean? | *Made to seem more serious than it really is | Made quieter | Praised loudly | Made to look bigger through a lens
exercise: «The speaker's voice was magnified by the microphone, so everyone at the back could hear.» What does magnified mean? | *Made louder and stronger | Made to look bigger on a screen | Made slower | Made smaller
test: In church, «My soul magnifies the Lord» means the singer is doing what to the Lord? | Making smaller | Hiding from | *Praising | Forgetting
test: A magnifying glass makes small print look what? | *Bigger | Smaller | Invisible | Blurry
exam: «The photo of the ant was greatly magnified, so it looked as big as a mouse.» What does greatly magnified mean? | Made quieter | *Made to look much bigger than it really is | Hidden | Made much smaller
